# Qdrant for the mirror - the Wikipedia mirror's passage index.
#
# A separate system user (`qdrant`) from the build user: the split is deliberate. The build job (wikimirror-sync) parses
# attacker-editable HTML and holds internet egress; if it were also the owner of the Qdrant
# files, a compromise of one parser would be a write to the index. Qdrant runs as `qdrant`,
# is reached only over loopback HTTP, and has no egress at all (nftables drops it).
#
# Loopback only, configured in /etc/wiki-mirror/qdrant.yaml (host, ports, storage,
# memory knobs - each justified there). Storage is on LOCAL SSD, never the NAS: Qdrant
# memory-maps its segments and misbehaves on NFS (docs/DESIGN.md section 4).
#
# Memory: this box has 32 GB (8 GB until 2026-10-17) for Qdrant, the API (1.5 G cap) and a build
# (5 G cap) together. MemoryMax is the hard stop that turns a runaway into a restart (Restart=on-failure) instead of
# an OOM of the whole container. Qdrant's mmap pages are reclaimable page cache, and at
# MemoryMax the kernel reclaims them first; only anonymous memory (about 1 GB while indexing)
# can trigger the kill.
#
# No MemoryHigh, deliberately. It was 3G until 2026-10-17: page cache counts toward it, so
# during the first full build Qdrant sat at the mark and the kernel throttled it 200,000 times.
# memory.high throttling *sleeps* the allocating task, which stretched the optimizer's segment
# rewrites while it held the segment locks; one upsert waited 144 s and the build died twice.
# memory.max reclaims without that penalty.
#
# MemoryMax went from 4G to 6G first: with the throttle gone, Qdrant was OOM-killed at 4G at
# 8.7M passages (2026-10-17 22:09), when concurrent segment merges (optimizer_cpu_budget: 3,
# qdrant.yaml) each built an inverted index on the heap. It recovered every segment from its
# WAL and the build resumed, so the failure is safe, but each one costs a restart.
#
# MemoryMax went to 12G, not 6G: the first full build finished (41.6M passages, 2026-10-18) with
# anonymous memory peaking at 5.1 GB during the last merges (one optimization at a time,
# 400 MB segments) and 4.3 GB at rest.
#
# MemoryMax was raised to 20G for the semantic index (W2) when it was to be held in RAM. That did
# not fit: on 2026-10-23, 4 h 38 min into the graph build with 62% of 41.6M passages indexed,
# Qdrant was OOM-killed at 20G. Its anonymous memory had been 19.2 GB at rest: graph links
# 6.87 GB (about 10.4 GB when complete), the pinned binary copy 5.32 GB, the keyword index 4.3 GB
# and about 2.7 GB of other per-point bookkeeping; finished it would have rested at about 22.7 GB.
#
# The rationale now (docs/DECISIONS.md D8): the semantic index lives entirely on disk - float16
# vectors, HNSW graph (`hnsw_config.on_disk`) and binary-quantized copy (`always_ram: false`) -
# memory-mapped, with the kernel's page cache keeping the hot parts in spare RAM and giving them
# back under pressure. Only anonymous memory can be OOM-killed. After the rebuild finished
# (2026-10-23) it rests at 7.4-7.5 GB (7.52 GB in the cgroup), under the goal of 10 GB pinned for
# the whole mirror.
#
# MemoryMax is 24G, and the reason is not the pinned memory. Under cgroup v2 the page cache that
# Qdrant's memory-mapped files bring in is charged to this cgroup, so the cap is also Qdrant's
# cache budget. Measured with a 26G runtime cap: memory.current sat at the cap (7.52 GB anonymous
# plus 19.93 GB file cache), memory.events showed `max` 13693 (cache reclaimed at the cap) and
# `oom_kill` 0. Reclaiming cache never kills, but a small cap means a small cache and slower
# semantic search; a 14G cap would leave about 6.5 GB of cache against about 10.4 GB of graph and
# 5.3 GB of binary copy that a search walks. The cap's job is to stop a runaway Qdrant before the
# container runs out of memory. On a 32 GB box, 24G is what the other units' caps leave: API 1.5G,
# build 5G, maintenance job 256M (the 2G embedding run never runs beside a build: they share the
# build lock) and about 1.25 GB for the system. A box with less RAM lowers the cap by the
# difference and pays in cache. The anonymous peak during a monthly build, when two keyword
# indexes exist side by side, is an estimate (12 GB) and still to be measured. A cap set at runtime
# with `systemctl set-property --runtime` lasts until reboot; this value applies after the next
# install.sh run or reboot.
#
# The maintenance job (wiki-mirror-maintain.timer) resumes a collection left paused by a restart,
# unless its crash-loop guard holds it, and wiki-mirror status and /v1/wiki/health show Qdrant's
# memory against this cap and say `degraded` above 90%.
#
# Install: install.sh does it. By hand:
#   cp wiki-mirror-qdrant.service /etc/systemd/system/ && systemctl daemon-reload
#   systemctl enable --now wiki-mirror-qdrant
#
# See docs/DESIGN.md sections 4 and 6, docs/OPERATIONS.md.

[Unit]
Description=Qdrant (mirror service passage index)
Documentation=file:///opt/wiki-mirror/docs/OPERATIONS.md
After=network.target

[Service]
Type=exec
User=qdrant
Group=qdrant
# Qdrant writes `.qdrant-initialized` into its working directory, so this must be writable.
WorkingDirectory=/var/lib/wiki-mirror/qdrant
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/qdrant --config-path /etc/wiki-mirror/qdrant.yaml
UMask=0077

Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=5s
# Let it flush its WAL; a hard kill mid-write costs an index repair on the next start.
TimeoutStopSec=120

MemoryMax=24G
# Memory-mapped segments: the default file limit is far too low for a corpus this size.
LimitNOFILE=65536

# Hardening. One writable path; no network beyond loopback listeners.
NoNewPrivileges=true
PrivateTmp=true
PrivateDevices=true
ProtectSystem=strict
ProtectHome=true
ProtectKernelTunables=true
ProtectKernelModules=true
ProtectControlGroups=true
RestrictSUIDSGID=true
RestrictRealtime=true
RestrictNamespaces=true
LockPersonality=true
SystemCallArchitectures=native
CapabilityBoundingSet=
ReadWritePaths=/var/lib/wiki-mirror/qdrant
RestrictAddressFamilies=AF_INET AF_INET6 AF_UNIX

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
